"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms=globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms||[]).push([[4829],{7463(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"tag":{"label":"Analysis","permalink":"/concepts/tags/analysis","allTagsPath":"/concepts/tags","count":3,"items":[{"id":"foundations-of-algorithms/complexity-analysis","title":"Complexity Analysis","description":"In the previous lesson we saw that measurements depend on the machine, the compiler and the implementation, and that comparing algorithms only by running them has serious limitations. We now develop the alternative: studying the algorithm on paper, by counting how many elementary operations it performs as a function of the size of its input.","permalink":"/concepts/foundations-of-algorithms/complexity-analysis"},{"id":"foundations-of-algorithms/complexity-of-loops","title":"Complexity of Loops","description":"Most iterative algorithms are, at their core, combinations of loops. Once you can determine quickly and reliably how many times a loop runs, you can analyze almost any iterative program. This lesson collects the most common patterns, explains how to derive each result and points out the traps that appear most often in exams and in real code.","permalink":"/concepts/foundations-of-algorithms/complexity-of-loops"},{"id":"foundations-of-algorithms/measuring-efficiency","title":"Measuring Efficiency","description":"In the case study we compared four strategies for the maximum pairwise product and saw differences ranging from seconds to fractions of a millisecond. But how exactly should we compare algorithms? Is it enough to run them and look at the clock? What should we measure, and how can we trust the numbers?","permalink":"/concepts/foundations-of-algorithms/measuring-efficiency"}],"unlisted":false}}')}}]);